            <div class="post-body__intro reveal">
              <p>Most contractor-hiring guides are written from the homeowner's seat. This one isn't. After 15 years and 135+ remodeling projects across Delaware County and the Main Line — Media, Swarthmore, Springfield, Havertown, Broomall — I've learned that the questions a contractor asks before starting a project matter just as much as the questions a homeowner asks when hiring one.</p>
              <p>This guide shares both sides. You'll get the questions we ask every client at ProBrothers before a single wall is touched, the questions you should be asking us (and any other contractor you're evaluating), the Pennsylvania-specific legal requirements that protect you under <strong>HICPA</strong> and the <strong>PA Uniform Construction Code</strong>, and realistic 2026 cost ranges for Delaware County — not U.S. national averages, but real numbers from the Philadelphia metro market.</p>
              <p>If you want the broad Pennsylvania contractor-law landscape, including HICPA registration requirements and licensing compliance, our post on <a href="/blog/top-general-contractors-in-pennsylvania/">top general contractors in Pennsylvania</a> covers those in depth. This article focuses on the practical, project-level decisions.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>What We Ask Every Client Before Starting — ProBrothers' Intake Process</h2>
              <p>Before we quote a project, we sit down with the client — in person or on a video call — and work through five questions. These aren't sales questions. They're the questions that determine whether our crew can actually deliver what the client is imagining.</p>

              <h3>1. What is the primary goal — function, aesthetics, or resale value?</h3>
              <p>These goals pull in different directions. A function-driven basement finishing project prioritizes waterproofing and electrical capacity over finishes. An aesthetics-driven kitchen remodel may justify higher-end cabinet faces even if the carcasses are builder-grade. A resale-focused renovation means we steer clients away from over-specifications that the local market won't price back in. Getting this answer first determines every material selection that follows.</p>

              <h3>2. What is your realistic budget — and does it include a 15–20% contingency?</h3>
              <p>In Delaware County, PA, older homes (particularly pre-1980 stock in Media, Springfield, and Swarthmore) frequently have surprises behind the walls: knob-and-tube electrical that must be brought to current PA UCC code, plumbing that needs re-routing, or structural framing that wasn't built to modern load standards. The contingency isn't optional — it's the difference between a smooth project and a scope renegotiation mid-demolition.</p>

              <h3>3. Do you have a hard deadline?</h3>
              <p>Holiday seasons, family events, and school-year starts are real constraints. We'd rather know your deadline on day one than try to compress the tail end of a project to hit a date we didn't know about. Rushed finishes are how projects that were 95% excellent end up with a single corner that bothers you for years.</p>

              <h3>4. Will the home be occupied during construction?</h3>
              <p>Occupied renovations change everything. We run HEPA-filtered dust containment, adjust work hours to fit your household schedule (including children and pets), and build a daily access/security protocol. For kitchen remodels where families need to eat, we set up a temporary kitchen station. This isn't extra — it's how we've operated since we started in Delaware County in 2009.</p>

              <h3>5. Which decisions do you want to make yourselves vs. delegate to us?</h3>
              <p>Some clients want to select every tile, fixture, and hardware finish. Others want to say "make it look great" and trust the team. Both are completely valid — but we need to know which you are before scheduling, because material selection decisions that come late are the number-one reason timelines slip.</p>

              <div class="post-callout">
                <p><strong>ProBrothers intake principle:</strong> A project where the goals, budget, timeline, and decision-making style are aligned from day one takes an average of 15–20% less elapsed time than an identical scope project where these aren't established upfront. We've seen this pattern across 135+ projects.</p>
              </div>

