                <p><strong>Client's primary goal:</strong> Transform a dated kitchen into a space that was both functional and visually striking — the client had a clear idea of the finish she wanted and made a distinctive two-tone cabinet choice.</p>
                <p><strong>What the intake process revealed:</strong> The client's brief was specific: she knew the materials, the style, and the colour split. That clarity meant our intake focused on sequencing — demo order, in-shop build timeline, and delivery staging — rather than design direction. Not every project needs extensive back-and-forth; what matters is knowing early exactly what the scope is and what is already decided.</p>
                <p><strong>Outcome:</strong> Kitchen went from old and dated to a standout renovation. The two-tone finish is a distinctive design choice that required precise cabinet manufacturing and finishing to execute well.</p>
                <p class="post-case-study__source"><em>Source: Project documentation, probrothers.com/projects/two-tone-kitchen-restoration/</em></p>
