            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Red Flags That Should Make You Walk Away</h2>

              <h3>Immediate disqualifiers</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>Cannot provide a HICPA registration number or registration is not verifiable at the PA AG's website</li>
                <li>Asks you to pull your own building permits</li>
                <li>Requests more than 25–30% upfront before any work begins</li>
                <li>Cannot or will not provide references from similar Delaware County projects</li>
                <li>Uses high-pressure sales tactics or pushes for same-day contract signing</li>
                <li>Provides a verbal estimate with no line-item written breakdown</li>
                <li>Has disciplinary actions or complaint history on their PA contractor license</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Yellow flags — investigate further before proceeding</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>Less than 3 years of documented business history in the local market</li>
                <li>Cannot clearly explain the scope or their proposed approach to your specific project</li>
                <li>Estimate is significantly lower than two other quotes (usually means missing scope, substituted materials, or future change-order strategy)</li>
                <li>Communication is already difficult or slow during the sales phase</li>
                <li>No clear workers' compensation coverage for crew members</li>
                <li>Cannot name the permit requirements for your specific project type in your township</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Common Mistakes Homeowners Make When Hiring Contractors</h2>

              <h3>Choosing on price alone</h3>
              <p>In 15 years in Delaware County, the lowest bid has almost never been the best value. Low bids either reflect missing scope (items the contractor assumes won't be needed but will be), substituted materials, or a business model built on change orders. Compare bids on scope alignment, not just the bottom line number.</p>

              <h3>Not getting everything in writing</h3>
              <p>Under HICPA, contracts for home improvement work over $500 in Pennsylvania must be in writing and must include specific elements: contractor's name and registration number, a description of the work, start and completion dates, and the total price. Even if PA law didn't require it, verbal agreements are worth nothing in a dispute. Every scope item, material specification, and milestone should be in the contract.</p>

              <h3>Paying too much upfront</h3>
              <p>Reasonable structure: 10–20% deposit to start, milestone-based progress payments (typically at demolition completion, rough-in completion, and finish installation start), 10–15% final payment on completion and walkthrough sign-off. Paying in full upfront removes your primary leverage for quality completion.</p>

              <h3>Skipping reference checks</h3>
              <p>Call the references. Ask: Was the project on budget? Did they meet the timeline? How did the crew handle unexpected issues? Would you hire them again? The answers to those questions tell you more than a 5-star Google review.</p>

              <h3>Not confirming permit requirements before signing</h3>
              <p>Unpermitted work creates problems you may not discover until you try to sell or refinance. Before signing any contract, confirm with the contractor exactly which permits are required for your project under PA UCC, who pulls them, and how permit inspections are scheduled and documented.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Your Delaware County Remodeling Contractor Checklist</h2>

              <h3>Research phase</h3>
