            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — Delaware County, PA Remodeling Contractors</h2>

              <h3>How do I verify a contractor's license in Pennsylvania?</h3>
              <p>In Pennsylvania, home improvement contractors are registered under HICPA (73 Pa. Stat. § 517.1). Verify any contractor's registration number and complaint history at the <a href="https://www.attorneygeneral.gov/protect-yourself/home-improvement-contractor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PA Attorney General's HIC contractor lookup</a>. This is the authoritative source — not the contractor's own website or a third-party directory.</p>

              <h3>Do I need a permit for my remodeling project in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
              <p>In Delaware County, PA UCC building permits are required for structural work (wall removal, additions), new electrical circuits or panel upgrades, plumbing modifications, deck and porch construction, and finished basement additions. Processing time is typically 2–4 weeks for residential projects. Individual townships (Media, Springfield, Haverford, Upper Darby) administer permits — timelines vary. Your licensed contractor should pull all permits in their name, not yours.</p>

              <h3>How much does a kitchen remodel cost in Delaware County, PA in 2026?</h3>
              <p>Based on Remodeling Magazine's Cost vs. Value Report (Philadelphia metro, 2026): budget kitchen remodel (refaced cabinets, laminate counters) runs $35,000–$55,000; mid-range full replacement runs $85,000–$130,000; upscale custom kitchen runs $150,000–$250,000+. In older Delaware County homes, non-standard electrical panels and cast-iron plumbing can add $5,000–$15,000 to the scope. Actual cost depends on your specific project scope, materials, and site conditions. ProBrothers provides free, detailed quotes within one business day.</p>

              <h3>What is HICPA and why does it matter for homeowners?</h3>
              <p>The PA Home Improvement Consumer Protection Act (73 Pa. Stat. § 517.1) requires all home improvement contractors in Pennsylvania to register with the State Attorney General's office, carry insurance, and provide written contracts for jobs over $500. Hiring an unregistered contractor removes your legal protections if the work is defective or incomplete. Our comprehensive guide to <a href="/blog/top-general-contractors-in-pennsylvania/">top general contractors in Pennsylvania</a> covers HICPA in detail.</p>

              <h3>How long does a bathroom remodel take in Delaware County?</h3>
              <p>A cosmetic bathroom refresh (new fixtures, paint, tile resurfacing) typically takes 2–3 weeks. A full gut remodel with new plumbing, tile, fixtures, and vanity takes 4–6 weeks. Older Delaware County homes with cast-iron plumbing or galvanized supply lines may require additional time for pipe replacement before tile work begins. PA UCC permits for plumbing work add 1–3 weeks for permit processing at the start of the project.</p>

              <h3>Should I get multiple quotes for my remodeling project?</h3>
              <p>Yes — get at least three written, line-item quotes. You're not just comparing bottom-line numbers: you're comparing scope interpretation (does each contractor include the same work?), materials specified, timeline commitments, and communication style. A significantly lower bid usually means missing scope or substituted materials — verify what's included before assuming you've found a deal.</p>

              <h3>What certifications should I look for in a Delaware County remodeling contractor?</h3>
              <p>In addition to PA HICPA registration: CGR (Certified Graduate Remodeler) from NARI demonstrates advanced project management knowledge; LEED certification indicates expertise in energy-efficient construction; membership in NARI or the Delaware County Home Builders' Association indicates commitment to professional standards. None of these substitute for verified insurance, local references, and a solid written contract.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Conclusion</h2>
              <p>Hiring the right remodeling contractor in Delaware County comes down to three things: verifying the credentials that Pennsylvania law requires, understanding the permit obligations specific to your project and township, and establishing goals, budget, and communication expectations before a single wall is touched.</p>
              <p>The 14 questions in this guide will separate professionals from pretenders. The PA UCC and HICPA information will protect you legally. And the Delaware County cost ranges will keep your budget in the realm of reality rather than national averages that don't reflect your local market.</p>
